                                   "Raw":  "# Dad\u0027s MMO Lab -- WSL2 resource limits. Safe to edit by hand; the installer\n# will not overwrite this file once it exists. If a WoW server build runs out\n# of memory, lower \u0027processors\u0027 and/or raise \u0027swap\u0027 (see the HOWTO).\n[wsl2]\nmemory=16GB\nprocessors=6\nswap=10GB\nlocalhostForwarding=true\nvmIdleTimeout=60000\n\n# THE ARITHMETIC, corrected 2026-08-01 after this machine crashed mid-build.\n#\n# AzerothCore\u0027s Dockerfile builds with `-j $(($(nproc) + 1))` -- read from the\n# pinned checkout, hardcoded inside the RUN with no ARG, so nothing outside the\n# image can change it. The Docker VM inherits the limits in this file, so:\n#\n#     processors = N   -\u003e   N + 1 concurrent C++ compilers   -\u003e   ~2 GB each\n#\n# The previous setting was processors=8 / memory=16GB, written on 2026-07-31\n# with a comment claiming it \"keeps ~2.0 GB per parallel compiler against the\n# 16GB above\". That was wrong by exactly one compiler: 8 processors starts\n# NINE, needing ~18 GB against 16. The build over-committed and the machine\n# went down.\n#\n# Now: 6 processors -\u003e 7 compilers -\u003e ~14 GB, inside 20 GB with real headroom\n# for the linker peak (larger than a compile job) and the MySQL container\n# running beside it. The host has 31.2 GB, so this still leaves ~11 GB for\n# Windows.\n#\n# THE RULE when changing either number: memory \u003e= (processors + 1) * 2 GB,\n# plus a few GB of headroom. Raise `memory` before `processors`, never after.\n#\n# NB `swap` is not a safety net for this. Swapping a C++ link step thrashes the\n# disk badly enough to take the desktop down with it, which is what happened.\n\n[experimental]\n# autoMemoryReclaim lives HERE, not under [wsl2]. In [wsl2] WSL rejected it\n# with \"Unknown key \u0027wsl2.autoMemoryReclaim\u0027\" on every single wsl.exe call --\n# so it was inert AND noisy.\nautoMemoryReclaim=gradual"
                               }
             },
