    "caveats":  [
                    "ATTRIBUTION: all WSL distros share ONE utility VM and ONE Linux kernel. The \u0027vmmemWSL\u0027 figure is the WHOLE VM, not this backend\u0027s share of it. It is attributable to \u0027desktop\u0027 only because the preconditions established that no other distro was running.",
                    "A memory cap is set in .wslconfig (memory=16GB). Any loaded reading at or near this cap is the CAP, not the demand: the workload may want more and be unable to take it. Do not read a capped figure as \u0027this backend needs N GB\u0027.",
                    "autoMemoryReclaim=gradual is set. Memory freed inside the VM returns to Windows GRADUALLY and on an undocumented schedule, so any single post-stop sample measures the reclaim schedule as much as the workload. Read the \u0027stopped\u0027 SERIES, never one point from it.",
                    "The machine-level figures include EVERYTHING running on the box, not just this backend. They are only meaningful against an \u0027off\u0027 baseline taken on the same quiet machine in the same sitting.",
                    "Distros Running at sample time: docker-desktop."
                ],
    "notMeasured":  [
                        "Per-distro memory. WSL2 runs every distro in ONE VM with ONE kernel, and /proc/meminfo is not namespaced, so a distro asked about memory reports the whole VM\u0027s. Two distros running at once cannot be told apart from Windows OR from inside. The harness handles this by REFUSING to sample when more than one is up, not by splitting the number.",
                        "The kernel\u0027s own share (page cache, slab, tmpfs) within the VM. It is real memory the backend causes to be used, but it belongs to the shared kernel and cannot be assigned to a distro.",
                        "Bots actually online. That needs SOAP or MySQL; -BotsOnline records the OPERATOR\u0027s claim and is not a measurement.",
                        "Disk actually freed. A .vhdx never shrinks, so the disk figures are high-water marks; \u0027docker system df\u0027 gives the engine\u0027s logical view, which is the comparable one.",
                        "Docker Desktop\u0027s Windows-side memory in the Arch column: it is zero only because Desktop is required to be exited. Any user who keeps Desktop installed and running for other work pays it on both backends."
                    ]
